WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:08.830
DAVID WEITZ: When you eat food, there's
much more to food than just the taste.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.500
In addition to the taste,
there's the texture,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.760
the way the food feels in your mouth.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.820
As a good example, this
steak that we have here.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.810
We're going to cook the steak, and
I'm going to show you how it feels--

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.770
how the way it feels
changes when you cook it.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.160
What that means is when
you chew on the steak, how

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.180
it feels when you chew on it changes.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.360
That's the texture.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.950
That's the mouthfeel.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.820
And that's determined by
the elasticity of the steak.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.370
The elasticity of the
steak will increase

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:47.050
as it becomes more difficult to chew as
we cook it for a longer period of time.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.190
So all foods have a texture.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.260
All foods have a mouthfeel.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.880
If you don't like steak, for example, if
you prefer tofu if you're a vegetarian,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.070
it too comes in different flavors.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.170
It comes in different styles.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.879
You can get a firm tofu
or you can get soft tofu.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:09.240
A firm tofu takes more force
from your mouth to chew it.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.310
It's more elastic.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.310
It's stiffer.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.800
A soft tofu is much softer.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.670
It's much easier to chew.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.900
And when you're finished the steak
and your tofu, you have dessert.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.050
You have chocolate.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.740
It too comes with different textures.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.720
Chocolate mousse-- it's light.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.160
It's airy.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.770
It's very easy to chew.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.380
It's a very low elastic material.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.530
The elasticity of chocolate
mousse is very low.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.250
If you take a chocolate
bar, it's stiffer.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.140
It takes more force to chew it.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.460
It's elasticity increases.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.320
And if you take real chunk chocolate,
a really good Belgian chocolate,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.760
it's much stiffer.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.720
You have to bite much harder.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:57.130
So the mouthfeel, how it tastes, all is
wrapped up in the elasticity of a food.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.670
And this is how we characterize
what the mouthfeel of a food is.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.420
So if we want to measure it, we
have to measure the elasticity.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.940
And let's start with
the very simplest thing.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.620
Let's start with a spring.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:14.360
Here I have a spring, and I can hang
a weight on the end of the spring.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.260
If I hang a weight on the end of
the spring, the spring lengthens.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.700
It becomes longer.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.310
It becomes longer by a certain amount.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:29.090
That amount-- that delta L-- the change
in length is determined by how stiff,

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.680
by how elastic, the spring is
and how large the weight is.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.270
So let me hang the weight on it.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.480
The spring gets longer,
but then it stops moving.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.220
The forces are balanced.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.500
There's no longer a net
force on the spring.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.060
The spring is exerting a
force to hold the weight up,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.360
and the weight is exerting a
force to stretch the spring.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.020
We can do the same thing if
instead of lengthening the spring,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.520
we compress the spring.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.910
We put the weight on top
of the spring like this.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.120
If you push on the spring
and you push it down,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.635
then the spring pushes upwards.

00:03:03.635 --> 00:03:06.180
It pushes in the opposite direction.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.040
It tries to bring itself
back to its original length.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.000
And we can write the
relationship between the force

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.140
of the spring and the change
in the length of the spring.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.730
It's called Hooke's Law.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.820
And it just says that
the force is linearly

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:26.120
proportional to the change in length.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:31.340
This minus sign arises because
there is always a restoring force.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.770
The spring is always trying
to retain its original length.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:40.760
It's trying to get longer,
if you're compressing it.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.300
And it's trying to get shorter,
if you're extending it.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.290
The relationship between the
change in length and the force

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.080
is the spring constant,
and that determines

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.540
the elasticity of the spring.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.930
The larger the spring constant
is, the more of the force

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.280
it takes to extend it by a given length.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.150
So a stiff spring has a
very large spring constant,

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.680
and it takes a larger
force to extend it.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:13.570
We can use the same concept
now to describe food.

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:16.420
In the case of food,
we have some object.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.790
For example, we have
this piece of steak.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:23.070
We want to measure the
elasticity of a piece of steak.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.050
The force that we put on the food--

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.820
the force that we put on the steak--

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.670
will depend on the size of the steak.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:34.080
So if I take a very large piece
of steak, and I want to deform it.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.440
I want to squeeze it.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.300
I want to change its
thickness by a certain amount.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.920
It takes a much larger force than
if I take a small piece of steak.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.760
But when I tell you what the
elasticity of the steak is,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.530
I don't want to have the dimensions
of the object, of the piece of steak,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.270
as part of the elasticity.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.740
So I want to somehow take out
the dimensions of the steak.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:03.810
So to do that, I take the
force and I normalize the force

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.800
by the area on which I apply the force.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.900
That gives me the stress.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.420
The same point, I don't want to know how
thick the steak is when I compress it.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.260
I just want to know how much I
compress it for a given thickness.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:21.660
So I take the compression--

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.460
I take the change in thickness,
as I squeeze on the steak,

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.360
and I normalize it by
the original length.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.660
And that gives me a strain.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.580
A strain is the relative
change in length.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.620
Now I can calculate the elastic
constant in the same way

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.930
that I calculated the spring constant.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.960
I want to calculate a spring
constant, I take a force

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.480
and then divide it by
a change in length.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.330
For the elastic constant
of the steak, I take

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.200
the stress, which is the
force divided by the area,

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.870
and I divide that by the strain, which
is the relative change in length.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:05.360
And that gives me the
elastic constant of the food.

